 10.42, "sigma_race": 4.5, "sigma_total": 5.17, "baseline_margin": 10.15, "analyst_adjustment": -0.65, "key_factors": ["Ossoff leads every recent poll by 7-15 points, including large-sample surveys", "Massive fundraising edge: $115M raised and $15M on hand vs. Collins' $11M and $1.1M", "Expert ratings cluster at Likely D or better, and markets price Ossoff at 97-98%", "Georgia's near-even partisan lean limits the upside, but the environment favors Democrats"], "rationale": "Every poll since September shows Ossoff ahead by at least seven points, with the latest large surveys at 7-15 points, and he holds an overwhelming financial advantage over Collins. Expert ratings and prediction markets agree on a comfortable Ossoff win, so only a modest discount from the polling average is warranted.", "watch": "A late turnout differential or a runoff dynamic under Georgia's 50% rule could tighten the race if Ossoff falls short of a majority.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Jon Ossoff (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Senator Jon Ossoff seeks a second term against Republican Representative Mike Collins in a state with an almost even partisan lean. Ossoff has dominated the money race, raising over $115 million and holding $15 million on hand, while Collins has raised about $11 million with just over $1 million available. Outside spending has been limited on both sides.\n\nPolling has been consistently favorable to Ossoff: every survey since mid-September shows him leading by seven to fifteen points, including a 2,500-voter InsiderAdvantage poll in early November and large YouGov samples. Expert raters mostly call the race Likely Democratic, with several rating it Safe or Solid, and prediction markets give Ossoff roughly a 97% chance. The forecast expects Ossoff to win by around nine to ten points.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9499, "margin": 8.78}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9449, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9229, "margin